      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, those \"stubborn\" or \"rogue\" frequencies are precisely where the chaotic nature of the system becomes most apparent, often manifesting as discrete, anomalous peaks in the spectrum. These are not simply broadened harmonics; they are often evidence of localized, high-energy non-linear events occurring at the lip-plate interface, which act as transient, localized oscillators. Think of them as spectral echoes of the micro-instabilities within the airflow—a sort of acoustic \"flicker\" that the linear model simply cannot resolve. These rogue tones are the sonic signature of the system momentarily escaping the stable, predictable mode of operation.",
      "source": "target",
