    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's interesting how you characterize them as the system \"escaping\" its stable mode. It almost sounds like these rogue frequencies have an agency of their own, popping in and out to disrupt the stability. In professional musicology or lutherie, is there any specific, even if perhaps slightly unscientific, terminology used to describe these types of elusive, obstructive acoustic anomalies? Like, do people ever use more colorful, or even folklore-adjacent metaphors, to capture the sense that these frequencies are little \"nuisances\" hiding within the instrument's resonance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the more practical, performance-oriented realms of musicology and lutherie, while the rigorous terms remain rooted in acoustics, the descriptive language often leans toward the evocative. When these disruptive frequencies appear, they are frequently colloquially described as \"wolf tones\" or \"wolf whistles,\" even if the physical mechanism is far more complex than simple sympathetic resonance. These terms capture the essence of a persistently irritating, unwanted overtone that seems to possess an independent, disruptive character. They are the acoustic equivalent of a persistent, nagging *squeak* that refuses to be silenced by standard embouchure adjustments.",
      "source": "target",
